WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.480
- Okay, so, Paul,
what are we looking at here?

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.320
This is 3ds Max?

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.955
- This is 3ds Max, yes.

00:00:05.955 --> 00:00:08.913
And what I've created 
is an incredibly simple,

00:00:08.913 --> 00:00:11.293
we'll call it a leg.

00:00:11.293 --> 00:00:12.973
And it has...

00:00:12.973 --> 00:00:15.197
You know, it's being driven
by the skin modifier

00:00:15.197 --> 00:00:16.975
and a bit of animation
on the bone.

00:00:16.975 --> 00:00:19.520
So it's trying
 to mimic the idea

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.055
of a leg bending
and flexing.

00:00:23.055 --> 00:00:26.015
But there's some
severe problems with this.

00:00:26.015 --> 00:00:28.021
Again, like we were
discussing before,

00:00:28.021 --> 00:00:30.790
is that we've got a lot more
complex things happening.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:32.428
And so trying to mimic

00:00:32.428 --> 00:00:34.399
skin moving over two bones

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:36.310
isn't what happens in us.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.310
There is a lot more going on.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.030
And so we run into problems.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.945
And one of them is

00:00:42.945 --> 00:00:44.837
what is called
linear skin weight.

00:00:44.837 --> 00:00:48.155
So we can see the problem
here, happening.

00:00:48.155 --> 00:00:51.330
As this leg goes down,
you can actually see the knee

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.435
getting narrower across this.

00:00:53.435 --> 00:00:56.095
So if you look at
this point here, specifically,

00:00:56.095 --> 00:00:58.480
that point is weighted 50-50

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.270
between the upper bone,

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:01.960
the thigh bone
and the calf bone.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.969
And you can see as it comes
 down, it actually gets

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:06.401
closer and closer
and closer to the knee,

00:01:06.401 --> 00:01:08.315
and slowly starts to collapse.

00:01:08.315 --> 00:01:10.052
- And you mentioned bone.
Is that...

00:01:10.052 --> 00:01:12.455
I see there's something
inside the model there.

00:01:12.455 --> 00:01:14.775
- So these are the bones
that are in here.

00:01:14.775 --> 00:01:16.471
So that's a bone there,

00:01:16.471 --> 00:01:18.148
and then this is
another bone here,

00:01:18.148 --> 00:01:20.855
and I can select them,
you know, and whatnot.

00:01:20.855 --> 00:01:22.486
I've got an what's called
an IK solver,

00:01:22.486 --> 00:01:24.021
something else
we can talk about.

00:01:24.021 --> 00:01:25.975
A little bit of trig going
on there for the animation.

00:01:25.975 --> 00:01:28.080
That point on the end,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.363
so if we take a look
at the skin modifier,

00:01:30.363 --> 00:01:33.702
the skin modifier
allows us to be able to

00:01:33.702 --> 00:01:36.855
essentially take
individual points.

00:01:36.855 --> 00:01:38.911
And in this, you know,
in this case,

00:01:38.911 --> 00:01:41.533
this one here, let's say,

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:44.455
and weight them 
between bones.

00:01:44.455 --> 00:01:47.495
So you, Scott, you mentioned
to be able to multiply matrices.

00:01:47.495 --> 00:01:50.242
Well, this bone, this vertex

00:01:50.242 --> 00:01:52.534
is actually tracking
both the thigh bone

00:01:52.534 --> 00:01:54.302
and the calf bone.

00:01:54.302 --> 00:01:56.920
And so it has to remember
 where it started

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.595
in relationship
to those two bones

00:01:59.595 --> 00:02:02.219
and store a matrix,
an offset matrix

00:02:02.219 --> 00:02:04.852
from the thigh bone,
an offset matrix from

00:02:04.852 --> 00:02:07.224
the calf bone,
and then it stores

00:02:07.224 --> 00:02:08.735
a weight between,

00:02:08.735 --> 00:02:11.055
a weight between those
two matrices.

00:02:11.055 --> 00:02:13.851
So when we see it bend,

00:02:13.851 --> 00:02:16.479
eventually,
you'll very noticeably

00:02:16.479 --> 00:02:18.015
start seeing it moved in.

00:02:18.015 --> 00:02:20.173
So I've actually come up
with a nice little display

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:22.140
of what's going on there.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.858
So we can think of,
I've got these

00:02:23.858 --> 00:02:27.260
three point helpers,
happening here.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.820
And these three
point helpers are set up.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.580
I'll just turn this back off.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.888
So this upper one,
 this red one here

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:37.885
is actually parented
to the upper bone.

00:02:37.885 --> 00:02:40.390
So you can think of
 that like the original

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.805
starting transform matrix
offset from this bone.

00:02:43.805 --> 00:02:47.371
So that vertex is storing
 this matrix value here,

00:02:47.371 --> 00:02:49.315
which is an offset
from that bone.

00:02:49.315 --> 00:02:52.170
- So when you say offset,
do you mean like a translation?

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.666
- Well, when I rotate it,

00:02:54.666 --> 00:02:56.523
you can see that one
I have selected

00:02:56.523 --> 00:02:58.090
is staying with
that upper bone.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:02:59.770
It's rotating
with that upper bone.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:01.558
The blue one on the bottom

00:03:01.558 --> 00:03:03.372
is parented to the calf bone,

00:03:03.372 --> 00:03:05.214
so it is moving
and rotating

00:03:05.214 --> 00:03:07.784
with the lower one, okay?

00:03:07.784 --> 00:03:10.861
And then the green one
 is what we call

00:03:10.861 --> 00:03:13.309
position constrained
in a linear fashion

00:03:13.309 --> 00:03:14.934
between the upper
and the lower one.

00:03:14.934 --> 00:03:17.287
So you can see it's staying,
in this case, that vertex

00:03:17.287 --> 00:03:19.030
has a 50-50 weighting.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.830
50 per cent to the upper bone,
50 per cent to the bottom.

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.764
So something else I guess
 to note here is that

00:03:23.764 --> 00:03:26.950
every vertex has
to have normalized weighting.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.153
So however many bones

00:03:29.153 --> 00:03:32.365
that affect that vertex
has to add up to one.

00:03:32.365 --> 00:03:35.898
Okay, so this is a,
you know, 50-50 split

00:03:35.898 --> 00:03:38.135
between these two,
 and you can see it's staying

00:03:38.135 --> 00:03:41.085
in a straight line between
these two points.

00:03:41.085 --> 00:03:44.028
So as this starts to rotate

00:03:44.028 --> 00:03:46.767
more and more,
you can see why

00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:49.398
it begins to collapse,
because it's in a straight line

00:03:49.398 --> 00:03:51.990
between the upper matrix
and the lower matrix.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.672
Basically, the thigh bone
matrix offset,

00:03:54.672 --> 00:03:57.917
we'll call it,
and the calf bone matrix offset,

00:03:57.917 --> 00:04:00.850
and it's staying weighted
between those two, okay?

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:03.593
- And you said that the top
and the bottom one

00:04:03.593 --> 00:04:05.275
are parented.

00:04:05.275 --> 00:04:07.675
Now, I think of my mom and dad
when I think of parents,

00:04:07.675 --> 00:04:09.595
so what do you mean by that?

00:04:09.595 --> 00:04:12.075
- As that display,
I just took this one here,

00:04:12.075 --> 00:04:14.555
and it is a child of this one.

00:04:14.555 --> 00:04:17.370
So it is inheriting
the transform matrix

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:19.495
of the thigh bone,

00:04:19.495 --> 00:04:22.718
with an offset value
from it, okay?

00:04:22.718 --> 00:04:24.908
And that's what happens in skin.

00:04:24.908 --> 00:04:27.474
So if I pick this skin here,

00:04:27.474 --> 00:04:29.003
and scroll on down,

00:04:29.003 --> 00:04:30.966
you can actually see
the weights of it.

00:04:30.966 --> 00:04:32.603
I'll just open this weight list.

00:04:32.603 --> 00:04:34.995
So with this weight
tool open, you can see

00:04:34.995 --> 00:04:36.617
with this vertex picked,
it's actually

00:04:36.617 --> 00:04:38.155
using those two bones.

00:04:38.155 --> 00:04:40.494
So it's actually weighted
between those two bones

00:04:40.494 --> 00:04:42.360
and it's weighted, well,
not quite a 50-50.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.877
It looks like I messed up.

00:04:43.877 --> 00:04:46.150
But we could actually set
that to a pure

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.590
50-50 split between those.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:51.662
So if we weighted those
differently, I said maybe 25,

00:04:51.662 --> 00:04:54.270
you can see that point
is now down here.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.697
It's because bone two has got

00:04:56.697 --> 00:04:59.015
0.75 weighting on it,

00:04:59.015 --> 00:05:01.215
and the top one is a 0.25.

00:05:01.215 --> 00:05:04.095
So it's actually pulled down
more with the bottom bone

00:05:04.095 --> 00:05:06.975
as that bottom bone goes.

00:05:06.975 --> 00:05:08.695
But again, back to
the 50-50 weighting

00:05:08.695 --> 00:05:11.577
and it's right in between.
So that's how that's done.

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:13.608
Now, there's a problem
with this that collapsing

00:05:13.608 --> 00:05:15.600
causes other issues.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.337
I'm just going to hide these

00:05:17.337 --> 00:05:19.120
and just show this guy here.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.036
This one kind of represents,

00:05:21.036 --> 00:05:22.798
well, think of it
like an arm,

00:05:22.798 --> 00:05:25.257
and this is a problem
we run into.

00:05:25.257 --> 00:05:27.490
You know, you've got
an arm, and we have

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:29.896
a hard rotation kind
of happening

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:32.215
up and down,
side to side.

00:05:32.215 --> 00:05:34.855
So we want our weight set up
so that it produces

00:05:34.855 --> 00:05:37.655
this nice hard crease here.

00:05:37.655 --> 00:05:40.346
The thing is, when we rotate
our hand over,

00:05:40.346 --> 00:05:42.742
our arm has an ulnar radius,

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:45.153
fat, muscle tissues,
all kinds of other stuff,

00:05:45.153 --> 00:05:47.147
and we get this
 nice soft blend all the way

00:05:47.147 --> 00:05:48.889
down the length of it, okay?

00:05:48.889 --> 00:05:51.623
So we're rotating
 effectively the same joint,

00:05:51.623 --> 00:05:53.948
but we want completely
different weighting.

00:05:53.948 --> 00:05:55.540
So what can happen is this.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.133
So you can see as we go down,
we get that nice, hard weight

00:05:58.133 --> 00:05:59.700
and it comes back up.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.331
But when we twist it,
 you'll notice that

00:06:02.331 --> 00:06:05.850
it does a candy-wrapper
effect, okay?

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:08.394
And that's because of
 that problem of those

00:06:08.394 --> 00:06:11.375
linear weights now,
 those two offset matrices

00:06:11.375 --> 00:06:14.225
are literally either side
of the joint,

00:06:14.225 --> 00:06:16.200
and so the vertex ends up
in the middle in

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.175
between the two, okay?

00:06:18.175 --> 00:06:19.955
So we have to come up

00:06:19.955 --> 00:06:22.030
with other ways of
dealing with that.

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:23.554
We need to cheat it.

00:06:23.554 --> 00:06:25.463
Now, you'll actually notice
there's multiple

00:06:25.463 --> 00:06:27.471
bones in here,

00:06:27.471 --> 00:06:29.240
in the forearm.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.440
So I've got the sort of
initial, you know,

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.465
forearm bone, but I've got
this extra one in here.

00:06:34.465 --> 00:06:37.636
And what this does is
it does a 50 per cent twist

00:06:37.636 --> 00:06:39.665
along only that X length.

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:41.745
So when the hand
twists over 180,

00:06:41.745 --> 00:06:43.825
it's only gone 90 degrees.

00:06:43.825 --> 00:06:47.262
And we skin to that as well
 so that what we can get is

00:06:47.262 --> 00:06:49.894
a way to try and just...

00:06:49.894 --> 00:06:52.500
It's still collapsing.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.340
It still has the linear weight.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:57.140
We're just making it
not as bad.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.251
So the more we do that,
the more we can look correct.

00:07:00.251 --> 00:07:02.032
- You're going to
like this part.

00:07:02.032 --> 00:07:04.516
The mathematics
to get that twist

00:07:04.516 --> 00:07:07.115
and all that,
dual quaternions.

00:07:07.115 --> 00:07:09.198
- Oh, wow.
 - That's how they're

00:07:09.198 --> 00:07:10.715
doing the mathematics
on this.

00:07:10.715 --> 00:07:12.682
So you don't get
the candy wrapper up.

00:07:12.682 --> 00:07:14.419
- Well, we've actually...
I haven't actually turned on

00:07:14.419 --> 00:07:16.687
dual quats, but I can even
turn on dual quats.

00:07:16.687 --> 00:07:18.599
- I see a button there.
- Yeah, there is

00:07:18.599 --> 00:07:20.343
a whole button for dual quats,

00:07:20.343 --> 00:07:23.578
so you can actually
take the vertices

00:07:23.578 --> 00:07:25.605
in the mesh.

00:07:25.605 --> 00:07:28.030
And if I were just
to paint that...

00:07:32.405 --> 00:07:34.474
And paint in

00:07:34.474 --> 00:07:36.186
dual quats into
that area...

00:07:36.186 --> 00:07:38.645
- Wow, so you're you're
painting the weight?

00:07:38.645 --> 00:07:40.960
- Painting the weight.
So it's a it's a way of sort of,

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.742
you know,
being able to input that.

00:07:42.742 --> 00:07:44.840
So that should work
even better now

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:46.805
as far as keeping the volume.

00:07:46.805 --> 00:07:48.808
I've got another little
problem coming up,

00:07:48.808 --> 00:07:50.644
but you can see it's
even collapsing less

00:07:50.644 --> 00:07:52.579
because it's using
dual quats now.

00:07:52.579 --> 00:07:55.067
Come to video games, though,
we generally don't do that,

00:07:55.067 --> 00:07:58.060
as far as I understand,
because of speed.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:01.306
Dealing with quaternions
is way more overhead

00:08:01.306 --> 00:08:03.154
than handling just, you know,

00:08:03.154 --> 00:08:05.397
a linear weighting
between points, right?

00:08:05.397 --> 00:08:07.562
So, it'll slow things down.

00:08:07.562 --> 00:08:10.221
But we use, in film production
and whatnot,

00:08:10.221 --> 00:08:11.935
we use methods like this.

00:08:11.935 --> 00:08:14.134
We track the length of edges,

00:08:14.134 --> 00:08:16.655
and then we drive things
with targets.

00:08:16.655 --> 00:08:19.649
There's all sorts of
layers of cheats

00:08:19.649 --> 00:08:21.934
because we don't have muscles

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:23.720
and we don't have whatnot.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:26.760
We can simulate muscle
simulations a bit.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.855
It only, you know,
it's like sometimes,

00:08:28.855 --> 00:08:30.554
cheats are just
the better way to do it.

00:08:30.554 --> 00:08:32.074
- Now, when you were
showing us the...

00:08:32.074 --> 00:08:33.757
You made a teapot,

00:08:33.757 --> 00:08:35.985
and it seemed very different
to what you're doing now.

00:08:35.985 --> 00:08:38.957
It's almost like a different
discipline or a different role.

00:08:38.957 --> 00:08:40.510
- It's a different role.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:42.577
The modeling is done
by modelers,

00:08:42.577 --> 00:08:45.048
sort of pure up
artists, generally,

00:08:45.048 --> 00:08:47.105
are creating the models.

00:08:47.105 --> 00:08:49.608
You know, the character that
would be driven here

00:08:49.608 --> 00:08:52.350
would have been
created by artists.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:54.709
And this is a tech art
position, really,

00:08:54.709 --> 00:08:56.815
where we have to try and solve

00:08:56.815 --> 00:08:59.659
all of the problems
of making things work, okay?

00:08:59.659 --> 00:09:01.950
Cassidy's a technical artist.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:04.711
You know, she's getting
all of this to work for us

00:09:04.711 --> 00:09:06.705
sort of thing.

00:09:06.705 --> 00:09:08.688
So we tend to come
from the art world,

00:09:08.688 --> 00:09:11.167
so we understand the art,

00:09:11.167 --> 00:09:13.831
but we're also dealing
with the math and the matrices

00:09:13.831 --> 00:09:15.497
and the understanding
of how things function,

00:09:15.497 --> 00:09:18.217
just not down that
deep rabbit hole

00:09:18.217 --> 00:09:20.122
of how do you actually
calculate a quaternion.

00:09:20.122 --> 00:09:22.287
- So we've been
talking about vertices

00:09:22.287 --> 00:09:24.170
or vertex positions.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.537
So I think of them
as a position

00:09:26.537 --> 00:09:28.810
of a corner of a triangle.

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:31.739
And looking at Paul's model
there, you've got quads.

00:09:31.739 --> 00:09:33.971
You represent them
using X, Y, Z,

00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:36.330
left, right,
up, down, in, out.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:39.115
But mathematicians and
coders were a little careful.

00:09:39.115 --> 00:09:41.736
We also put another
component, the W.

00:09:41.736 --> 00:09:43.915
And typically,
you just set W to one.

00:09:43.915 --> 00:09:45.915
That means you can translate.

00:09:45.915 --> 00:09:48.575
We go from model space,
where the vertices

00:09:48.575 --> 00:09:50.469
are built by the artist,

00:09:50.469 --> 00:09:52.650
and we hop over
to world space.

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:55.735
We've got a scale,
rotate, translate,

00:09:55.735 --> 00:09:58.490
and that's called
the model matrix.

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:00.403
But there's all sorts of
things you can

00:10:00.403 --> 00:10:02.170
mess around with.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:04.291
Vertices or the shaders

00:10:04.291 --> 00:10:06.155
that Scott talked about, yes,

00:10:06.155 --> 00:10:07.809
and also skinning.

00:10:07.809 --> 00:10:10.435
- Yep, it's all stored
in the vertex as, you know,

00:10:10.435 --> 00:10:13.035
those data within
each vertex.

00:10:13.035 --> 00:10:15.355
So we use vertex colours.

00:10:15.355 --> 00:10:17.344
We colour things
with vertices sometimes.

00:10:17.344 --> 00:10:18.980
- Wow, because I think of them
as position,

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:20.616
but also a colour.

00:10:20.616 --> 00:10:22.490
- Well, add a UVW, right?

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.970
So where a pixel will end up
on a surface, right,

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.450
is stored in a vertex.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.434
- It's easy.
- Yeah, it's easy.